# Tests that `tests/run.sh --target linux-arm64 --emu build/a64emu` builds but does not run:
# what the emulator (tests/arm64/emu.al) does not provide. "dir/name reason". (They are still
# built in their three modes.) The tests that print stack traces are not here: they fail until
# the arm64 back end makes the stack-trace table, which is work still to do.
run/emit_asm runs the compiler (a new process)
run/frame_too_large runs the compiler (a new process)
run/link_too_large runs the compiler (a new process)
run/lib_alloc_limits runs itself as child processes
run/lib_fmtf_negative runs itself as child processes
run/lib_stdout_errors runs itself as child processes (fork, dup2) and writes to /dev/full
run/lib_args_empty_argv runs itself as a child process (execve)
run/lib_read_all_size reads /proc files and pipes of child processes
run/thread_print runs itself as a child process
run/check_memory_stale ALC_CHECK_MEMORY takes pages away with mmap(MAP_FIXED, PROT_NONE), which the emulator does not
run/when_platform calls the x86-64 Linux system call getpid (39) by its number
std/asset reads /dev/null and /proc, which a Windows host does not have
std/asset_cloexec children, a FIFO and /proc
std/asset_io_error reads a /proc file
std/asset_user_dirs runs itself as child processes with changed environments
std/os starts processes
std/os_files the long-path case fails on Windows (being fixed elsewhere) and the emulator reports ENOENT for a path below a file
std/os_run starts processes
std/os_shm memfd_create and file maps
std/os_spawn starts processes
std/os_spawn_threads starts processes
std/portable_launch starts processes
std/portable_process starts processes
unit/basic test blocks run in child processes (fork): not emulated
unit/priv_tests test blocks run in child processes (fork): not emulated
std/time_huge starts processes
run/lib_find_linear too slow under the emulator for the 60 s limit
run/lib_sort_organ too slow under the emulator for the 60 s limit (-O0)
